/* Mock paper 2. Same format as paper 1. */
window.MOCK_PAPERS = window.MOCK_PAPERS || [];
window.MOCK_PAPERS.push({id:'p2',title:'Mock paper 2',sub:'A second full paper with different scenarios',qs:[
/* ---- upper limb ---- */
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'A 58-year-old painter has shoulder pain when lifting his arm sideways between about 60° and 120°, but not below or above this range. Which tendon is being impinged, and against what?',
 o:['Long head of biceps against the coracoid process','Supraspinatus against the coracoacromial arch','Infraspinatus against the spine of the scapula','Subscapularis against the lesser tubercle','Teres minor against the glenoid labrum'],a:1,
 e:'A painful arc in mid-abduction is typical of subacromial impingement: the supraspinatus tendon and subacromial bursa are squeezed between the greater tubercle and the coracoacromial arch (acromion and coracoacromial ligament). Pain at the top of abduction (above 120°) suggests acromioclavicular joint disease.'},
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'You are taking blood from the median cubital vein. Which structure lies deep to the vein and protects the brachial artery and median nerve?',
 o:['Biceps tendon','Brachioradialis','Bicipital aponeurosis','Pronator teres','Medial intermuscular septum'],a:2,
 e:'The bicipital aponeurosis is a fibrous sheet from the biceps tendon to the deep fascia of the medial forearm. It separates the superficial median cubital vein from the brachial artery and median nerve beneath it.'},
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'A man can extend his wrist, though it deviates radially, but cannot extend his fingers at the MCP joints. Sensation in the hand is normal. Where is the lesion?',
 o:['Radial nerve in the axilla','Radial nerve in the radial groove','Posterior interosseous nerve','Superficial branch of the radial nerve','C7 nerve root'],a:2,
 e:'The posterior interosseous nerve (deep branch of the radial nerve, through supinator) supplies finger and thumb extensors and ECU. Extensor carpi radialis longus is supplied by the radial nerve before it divides, so wrist extension survives but pulls radially. The PIN has no cutaneous branch, so sensation is normal.'},
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'A 72-year-old woman falls on her outstretched hand. Her wrist has a "dinner-fork" deformity. What does the X-ray most likely show?',
 o:['Distal radius fracture with volar displacement (Smith\'s)','Extra-articular distal radius fracture with dorsal displacement and angulation (Colles\')','Intra-articular fracture of the distal radius with carpal subluxation (Barton\'s)','Fracture of the radial styloid (Chauffeur\'s)','Fracture of the scaphoid waist'],a:1,
 e:'Colles\' fracture is an extra-articular fracture within about 2.5 cm of the wrist with dorsal displacement and angulation of the distal fragment, typical of osteoporotic bone after a fall on an outstretched hand. The median nerve can be compressed acutely.'},
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'Before inserting a radial arterial line, the doctor performs Allen\'s test. What is the test assessing?',
 o:['Whether the radial pulse is strong enough to cannulate','Whether the ulnar artery can supply the hand through the palmar arches if the radial artery blocks','Whether the brachial artery bifurcates high in the arm','Whether venous return from the hand is adequate','Whether the median nerve is intact'],a:1,
 e:'Both arteries are compressed until the hand blanches, then the ulnar artery is released. Quick return of colour shows adequate collateral flow through the superficial palmar arch (mainly ulnar) and deep palmar arch (mainly radial). This reduces the risk of hand ischaemia if the radial artery thromboses.'},
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'A stab wound in the axilla damages the posterior cord of the brachial plexus. Which muscle would still work normally?',
 o:['Deltoid','Latissimus dorsi','Triceps brachii','Subscapularis','Biceps brachii'],a:4,
 e:'The posterior cord gives the upper and lower subscapular nerves (subscapularis, teres major), thoracodorsal nerve (latissimus dorsi), axillary nerve (deltoid, teres minor) and radial nerve (triceps and the extensors). Biceps is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve from the lateral cord.'},
{t:'v',s:'ul',q:'Which tendon runs in the intertubercular (bicipital) groove of the humerus?',
 a:['long head of biceps','biceps long head','long head of biceps brachii','long head biceps','tendon of long head of biceps'],req:['long','biceps'],m:'Long head of biceps brachii',
 e:'The long head of biceps arises from the supraglenoid tubercle, runs through the shoulder joint inside the capsule, then down the intertubercular groove held by the transverse humeral ligament.'},
{t:'v',s:'ul',q:'Which carpal bone articulates with the base of the thumb metacarpal at the saddle-shaped first carpometacarpal joint?',
 a:['trapezium','the trapezium'],m:'Trapezium',
 e:'The trapezium forms a saddle joint with the first metacarpal, allowing opposition. It is a common site of osteoarthritis in older women. Do not confuse it with the trapezoid, which articulates with the second metacarpal.'},
{t:'v',s:'ul',q:'In Froment\'s sign, a patient grips paper between thumb and index finger and the thumb IP joint flexes. Weakness of which muscle does this reveal?',
 a:['adductor pollicis','adductor pollicis muscle','add pollicis'],m:'Adductor pollicis (ulnar nerve)',
 e:'Adductor pollicis (deep branch of the ulnar nerve) normally holds the paper. When it is weak, the patient substitutes flexor pollicis longus (anterior interosseous nerve), flexing the thumb IP joint.'},
/* ---- lower limb ---- */
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'A woman has varicose veins due to incompetence at the junction where the great saphenous vein joins the deep system. Through which opening does the great saphenous vein pass to reach the femoral vein?',
 o:['Adductor hiatus','Femoral ring','Saphenous opening in the fascia lata','Deep inguinal ring','Obturator canal'],a:2,
 e:'The great saphenous vein pierces the cribriform fascia over the saphenous opening in the fascia lata, about 3–4 cm inferolateral to the pubic tubercle, to join the femoral vein in the femoral triangle.'},
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'A driver hits his knee on the dashboard in a collision. His hip is dislocated posteriorly. How is the limb likely to lie, and which nerve is at greatest risk?',
 o:['Shortened, abducted and externally rotated; femoral nerve','Shortened, flexed, adducted and internally rotated; sciatic nerve','Lengthened, abducted and externally rotated; obturator nerve','Shortened and externally rotated; superior gluteal nerve','Normal length, internally rotated; femoral nerve'],a:1,
 e:'Most hip dislocations are posterior. The femoral head is pushed back behind the acetabulum, so the leg is shortened, flexed, adducted and internally rotated. The sciatic nerve runs just behind the joint and is injured in up to 10–20% of cases, usually its fibular division.'},
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'A pregnant woman has burning numbness over the outer thigh with no weakness and normal reflexes. Which nerve is compressed?',
 o:['Femoral nerve','Lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh','Genitofemoral nerve','Obturator nerve','Common fibular nerve'],a:1,
 e:'Meralgia paraesthetica: the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh (L2–3), a purely sensory nerve, is compressed as it passes under or through the inguinal ligament near the ASIS. Weight gain, pregnancy and tight belts are risk factors.'},
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'A 45-year-old feels a "kick" in the back of the ankle while playing badminton. Calf squeeze (Simmonds\') test shows no plantarflexion, yet he can still weakly point his foot. Why?',
 o:['The plantaris tendon alone is strong enough to plantarflex','Tibialis posterior, the long toe flexors and fibularis muscles can still plantarflex the ankle','The soleus attaches separately to the calcaneus','Tibialis anterior reverses its action','The rupture is only partial'],a:1,
 e:'Gastrocnemius and soleus act through the calcaneal tendon, but tibialis posterior, flexor hallucis longus, flexor digitorum longus and fibularis longus and brevis cross the ankle behind its axis and can weakly plantarflex it. Active plantarflexion therefore does not exclude a complete rupture, which is why Simmonds\' test is used.'},
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'A displaced supracondylar fracture of the femur endangers the deepest structure in the popliteal fossa. Which is it?',
 o:['Tibial nerve','Popliteal vein','Popliteal artery','Common fibular nerve','Small saphenous vein'],a:2,
 e:'From superficial to deep in the popliteal fossa: tibial nerve, popliteal vein, popliteal artery (lying on the femur and joint capsule). The artery is tethered at the adductor hiatus above and the soleal arch below, so it is easily torn by distal femoral fractures and knee dislocations.'},
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'After a pelvic lymph node dissection a woman has weak hip adduction and numbness over a small area of the medial thigh. Which nerve is injured?',
 o:['Femoral nerve','Obturator nerve','Genitofemoral nerve','Ilioinguinal nerve','Pudendal nerve'],a:1,
 e:'The obturator nerve (L2–4) runs along the lateral pelvic wall to the obturator canal, close to the pelvic lymph nodes. It supplies the medial compartment adductors and the skin of the medial thigh.'},
{t:'v',s:'ll',q:'A new army recruit develops forefoot pain after long marches. A stress ("march") fracture is most commonly found in which bone?',
 a:['second metatarsal','2nd metatarsal','second metatarsal shaft','metatarsal 2','2nd metatarsal bone','the second metatarsal'],m:'Second metatarsal',
 e:'The second metatarsal is long and fixed firmly at its base, so repetitive loading causes stress fractures of its shaft (the third is next most common). Early X-rays may be normal.'},
{t:'v',s:'ll',q:'Which nerve passes through the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus?',
 a:['tibial nerve','tibial','posterior tibial nerve'],m:'Tibial nerve',
 e:'Under the flexor retinaculum from front to back: Tibialis posterior, flexor Digitorum longus, posterior tibial Artery and vein, tibial Nerve, flexor Hallucis longus ("Tom, Dick And Very Nervous Harry"). Compression causes tarsal tunnel syndrome with burning in the sole.'},
{t:'v',s:'ll',q:'Name the strongest ligament of the hip joint, a Y-shaped band that prevents hyperextension.',
 a:['iliofemoral ligament','iliofemoral','ligament of bigelow','y ligament','y ligament of bigelow','iliofemoral ligament of bigelow'],m:'Iliofemoral ligament',
 e:'The iliofemoral ligament runs from the anterior inferior iliac spine to the intertrochanteric line and tightens in extension, letting us stand upright with little muscle effort.'},
/* ---- general MSK ---- */
{t:'s',s:'msk',q:'In compact bone, which channels run perpendicular to the long axis and connect neighbouring central (Haversian) canals?',
 o:['Canaliculi','Volkmann\'s (perforating) canals','Lacunae','Howship\'s lacunae','Interstitial lamellae'],a:1,
 e:'Volkmann\'s canals carry vessels from the periosteum and marrow into the central canals and link osteons together. Canaliculi are tiny channels connecting osteocytes; Howship\'s lacunae are resorption pits under osteoclasts.'},
{t:'s',s:'msk',q:'Which sequence best describes the stages of secondary (callus) fracture healing?',
 o:['Remodelling → haematoma → hard callus → soft callus','Haematoma and inflammation → soft (fibrocartilaginous) callus → hard (woven bone) callus → remodelling','Soft callus → haematoma → remodelling → hard callus','Haematoma → hard callus → soft callus → remodelling','Inflammation → remodelling → soft callus → hard callus'],a:1,
 e:'Bleeding forms a haematoma and inflammation brings in cells; granulation tissue and fibrocartilage form a soft callus; osteoblasts replace this with woven bone (hard callus) by endochondral ossification; finally lamellar bone replaces woven bone during remodelling over months to years.'},
{t:'s',s:'msk',q:'Soleus is an endurance postural muscle that is active whenever you stand. Which fibre type predominates in it?',
 o:['Type I (slow oxidative)','Type IIa (fast oxidative–glycolytic)','Type IIx (fast glycolytic)','Equal mix of all three','Intrafusal fibres'],a:0,
 e:'Postural muscles such as soleus are mostly type I fibres: slow, rich in mitochondria, myoglobin and capillaries, and resistant to fatigue. Gastrocnemius has more fast fibres for jumping and sprinting.'},
{t:'s',s:'msk',q:'A housebound 80-year-old has bone pain and proximal muscle weakness. Which blood results are most consistent with osteomalacia?',
 o:['Normal calcium, normal phosphate, normal ALP, normal PTH','High calcium, low phosphate, high PTH','Low or normal calcium, low phosphate, high ALP, high PTH','High calcium, high phosphate, low PTH','Normal calcium, high phosphate, low ALP'],a:2,
 e:'Vitamin D deficiency reduces gut absorption of calcium and phosphate. Secondary hyperparathyroidism keeps calcium near normal but drives phosphate loss, and poorly mineralised osteoid raises ALP. Osteoporosis typically has entirely normal bloods.'},
{t:'v',s:'msk',q:'Which regulatory protein lies along actin and covers the myosin-binding sites when muscle is at rest?',
 a:['tropomyosin'],m:'Tropomyosin',
 e:'Tropomyosin blocks the binding sites. When Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, the troponin complex shifts tropomyosin away, exposing the sites for cross-bridge formation.'},
{t:'v',s:'msk',q:'What type of synovial joint is the median atlantoaxial joint, which lets you shake your head "no"?',
 a:['pivot','pivot joint','trochoid','trochoid joint'],m:'Pivot (trochoid) joint',
 e:'The dens of C2 rotates within a ring formed by the anterior arch of C1 and the transverse ligament. About half of cervical rotation happens here.'},
/* ---- nervous ---- */
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A 42-year-old with a large central L4/5 disc prolapse has bilateral sciatica. Which finding most strongly suggests cauda equina syndrome needing emergency MRI?',
 o:['Positive straight leg raise at 40°','Reduced knee jerk on one side','Reduced perianal sensation with difficulty passing urine','Pain worse on coughing','Weak great toe extension on one side'],a:2,
 e:'Saddle anaesthesia and bladder (or bowel) dysfunction indicate compression of the sacral roots (S2–4) in the cauda equina. Without urgent decompression the sphincter damage can be permanent. The other findings occur in ordinary radiculopathy.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A 70-year-old has a painful vesicular rash in a band around his trunk at the level of the umbilicus. Varicella zoster virus has reactivated in which dorsal root ganglion?',
 o:['T4','T6','T10','L1','L3'],a:2,
 e:'The umbilicus lies in the T10 dermatome. Useful landmarks: nipple T4, xiphoid T6, umbilicus T10, inguinal ligament L1.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A 65-year-old smoker has a mild ptosis, a small pupil and a dry face on the left side, with wasting of the small muscles of the left hand. What is the most likely cause?',
 o:['Left posterior communicating artery aneurysm','Tumour at the apex of the left lung','Left C5 radiculopathy','Lesion of the left facial nerve','Carpal tunnel syndrome'],a:1,
 e:'A Pancoast tumour at the lung apex invades the sympathetic chain and stellate ganglion (Horner\'s syndrome: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) and the lower brachial plexus (T1), causing wasting of the intrinsic hand muscles.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A patient with a left hemisphere stroke has right facial weakness but can still wrinkle the right side of the forehead. Why is the forehead spared?',
 o:['The forehead is supplied by the trigeminal nerve','The part of the facial nucleus supplying the upper face receives input from both cerebral hemispheres','The facial nerve has already left the brainstem','Frontalis is supplied by the oculomotor nerve','The stroke affected only the lower part of the facial nucleus'],a:1,
 e:'Upper motor neurons from both hemispheres project to the part of the facial motor nucleus supplying the upper face, but mainly the contralateral hemisphere supplies the lower face. A UMN lesion therefore spares the forehead. A lower motor neuron lesion (e.g. Bell\'s palsy) affects the whole side of the face.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A man with a tumour in his right cerebellar hemisphere is examined. On which side will the intention tremor and dysdiadochokinesia be found?',
 o:['Left side only','Right side','Both sides equally','Left arm and right leg','No limb signs; only nystagmus'],a:1,
 e:'Each cerebellar hemisphere controls coordination on the same side of the body (its outputs cross and the descending motor pathways cross again). Midline (vermis) lesions cause truncal and gait ataxia.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'An 81-year-old who drinks heavily becomes slowly more confused over three weeks after a minor fall. CT shows a crescent-shaped collection over the left hemisphere. Which vessels were torn?',
 o:['Middle meningeal artery','Bridging veins crossing the subdural space','Berry aneurysm in the circle of Willis','Lenticulostriate arteries','Superior sagittal sinus'],a:1,
 e:'A chronic subdural haematoma comes from bridging veins torn as they cross from the cortex to the dural sinuses. Brain atrophy (age, alcohol) stretches them, so minor trauma is enough. The collection follows the brain surface (crescent). An extradural haematoma from the middle meningeal artery is lens-shaped and develops quickly.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A man eats home-canned food and develops a descending flaccid paralysis with dilated pupils. How does the toxin act?',
 o:['Blocks postsynaptic nicotinic receptors','Inhibits acetylcholinesterase','Cleaves SNARE proteins needed for acetylcholine release','Blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in axons','Destroys anterior horn cells'],a:2,
 e:'Botulinum toxin is a protease that cleaves SNARE proteins (SNAP-25, synaptobrevin, syntaxin), preventing vesicles fusing and releasing ACh at neuromuscular and autonomic cholinergic synapses. Anticholinesterases (organophosphates) cause the opposite, cholinergic excess.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'Why does hyperkalaemia make excitable cells (such as cardiac myocytes) depolarise at rest?',
 o:['It opens voltage-gated sodium channels','It reduces the K⁺ concentration gradient across the membrane, making the resting potential less negative','It stimulates the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase','It increases chloride permeability','It blocks calcium channels'],a:1,
 e:'The resting membrane potential is close to the K⁺ equilibrium potential because the membrane is mainly permeable to K⁺. A higher extracellular K⁺ reduces the gradient (Nernst equation), so the resting potential becomes less negative. Sustained depolarisation then inactivates Na⁺ channels, which is why severe hyperkalaemia causes arrhythmias.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A 60-year-old with vitamin B12 deficiency is steady with eyes open but sways and nearly falls when he closes them. Finger–nose test is normal. Which pathway is affected?',
 o:['Spinothalamic tracts','Dorsal columns','Lateral corticospinal tracts','Vestibular nuclei only','Cerebellar vermis'],a:1,
 e:'A positive Romberg\'s test means the patient relies on vision to compensate for lost proprioception: sensory ataxia from dorsal column (or large-fibre peripheral nerve) damage. In B12 deficiency, subacute combined degeneration affects the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts. In cerebellar ataxia the patient is unsteady even with eyes open.'},
{t:'v',s:'nerv',q:'Which artery runs on the inner surface of the skull deep to the pterion and is torn in an extradural haematoma?',
 a:['middle meningeal artery','middle meningeal','mma','anterior branch of middle meningeal artery'],m:'Middle meningeal artery',
 e:'The pterion, where frontal, parietal, temporal and sphenoid bones meet, is thin. A blow there can tear the anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery, causing an extradural haematoma, often after a lucid interval.'},
{t:'v',s:'nerv',q:'A patient cannot move their right eye outwards (abduct it) and complains of horizontal double vision. Which cranial nerve is affected?',
 a:['abducens','abducens nerve','cn vi','cn6','vi','6','sixth cranial nerve','abducent nerve','abducent'],m:'Abducens nerve (CN VI)',
 e:'The abducens nerve supplies lateral rectus. It has a long intracranial course over the petrous temporal bone, so it can be stretched by raised intracranial pressure (a "false localising sign").'},
{t:'v',s:'nerv',q:'In which part of the internal capsule do the corticospinal fibres run?',
 a:['posterior limb','posterior limb of internal capsule','the posterior limb','posterior limb of the internal capsule'],req:['posterior'],m:'Posterior limb',
 e:'Corticospinal fibres pass through the posterior limb (corticobulbar fibres near the genu). It is supplied by lenticulostriate branches of the MCA; a small lacunar infarct here causes a pure motor hemiparesis of face, arm and leg.'},
/* ---- cardiovascular ---- */
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'A 55-year-old has crushing chest pain and ST elevation in V1–V4. Which artery is most likely occluded?',
 o:['Right coronary artery','Circumflex artery','Left anterior descending artery','Posterior descending artery','Right marginal artery'],a:2,
 e:'V1–V4 look at the septum and anterior wall of the left ventricle, supplied by the LAD. Proximal LAD occlusion threatens a large territory, including the anterior two-thirds of the septum with the bundle branches.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'A woman has a pansystolic murmur loudest at the apex and radiating to the left axilla. Which valve lesion is most likely?',
 o:['Aortic stenosis','Aortic regurgitation','Mitral stenosis','Mitral regurgitation','Pulmonary stenosis'],a:3,
 e:'Mitral regurgitation produces a pansystolic murmur at the apex, radiating to the axilla, because blood leaks back into the left atrium throughout systole. Mitral stenosis gives a mid-diastolic rumble; aortic stenosis an ejection systolic murmur to the carotids.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'Why can cardiac muscle not be tetanised by rapid stimulation?',
 o:['It lacks troponin','It has a long refractory period due to the plateau phase of its action potential','It has no sarcoplasmic reticulum','Its gap junctions block repeated impulses','It relies entirely on extracellular calcium'],a:1,
 e:'The plateau (phase 2: L-type Ca²⁺ inflow balanced by K⁺ outflow) keeps the cell refractory until contraction is almost over, so a new contraction cannot summate with the previous one. This guarantees the heart relaxes and fills between beats.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'A man stands up quickly from lying down. Which mechanism keeps his blood pressure up within seconds?',
 o:['Renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system','ADH release','Baroreceptor reflex increasing sympathetic and reducing vagal output','Atrial natriuretic peptide release','Pressure natriuresis'],a:2,
 e:'Standing pools blood in the legs, lowering venous return and blood pressure. Reduced stretch of carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors lowers their firing; the medulla increases sympathetic output (heart rate, contractility, vasoconstriction) and reduces vagal tone within seconds. Hormonal responses take minutes or longer.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'A patient\'s pulse is irregularly irregular and the ECG has no P waves. Which wave of the jugular venous pulse will be absent?',
 o:['a wave','c wave','v wave','x descent','y descent'],a:0,
 e:'The "a" wave is produced by right atrial contraction. In atrial fibrillation there is no coordinated atrial contraction, so the a wave disappears. Cannon a waves occur when the atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve (e.g. complete heart block).'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'A patient has end-diastolic volume 120 mL and end-systolic volume 50 mL. What is the ejection fraction?',
 o:['42%','50%','58%','70%','71%'],a:2,
 e:'Stroke volume = 120 − 50 = 70 mL. Ejection fraction = SV / EDV = 70/120 ≈ 58%, within the normal range (about 55–70%).'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'A 17-year-old has hypertension in the arms, weak delayed femoral pulses and notching of the lower borders of the ribs on X-ray. Which vessels have enlarged to carry blood around the narrowing?',
 o:['Azygos and hemiazygos veins','Internal thoracic and intercostal arteries','Vertebral and basilar arteries','Pulmonary and bronchial arteries','Superior and inferior epigastric veins'],a:1,
 e:'In coarctation of the aorta, blood bypasses the narrowing via subclavian → internal thoracic → anterior intercostal → posterior intercostal arteries → descending aorta. The enlarged, tortuous intercostal arteries erode the inferior borders of the ribs. (Superior and inferior epigastric arteries also form collaterals.)'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'What is the earliest visible lesion of atherosclerosis?',
 o:['Fibrous cap','Fatty streak made of lipid-laden macrophages (foam cells)','Calcified plaque','Thrombus on a ruptured plaque','Aneurysmal dilatation'],a:1,
 e:'After endothelial injury, LDL enters the intima and is oxidised. Monocytes enter, become macrophages, engulf oxidised LDL and become foam cells, forming fatty streaks (seen even in children). Smooth muscle migration and collagen later form a fibrous cap over a lipid core.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'A man is stabbed just to the left of the sternum in the 4th intercostal space. Which heart chamber is most likely to be penetrated?',
 o:['Right atrium','Left atrium','Right ventricle','Left ventricle','Ascending aorta'],a:2,
 e:'The right ventricle forms most of the anterior (sternocostal) surface of the heart. The left atrium is posterior, and the left ventricle forms the left border and apex.'},
{t:'v',s:'cvs',q:'Name the fibrous cords that connect the papillary muscles to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.',
 a:['chordae tendineae','chordae','chordae tendinae','chorda tendinea'],m:'Chordae tendineae',
 e:'During ventricular systole papillary muscles contract and tighten the chordae tendineae, stopping the cusps everting into the atria. Papillary muscle rupture after MI causes acute mitral regurgitation.'},
{t:'v',s:'cvs',q:'In which chamber of the heart is the sinoatrial node located?',
 a:['right atrium','the right atrium','ra','wall of right atrium'],m:'Right atrium (near the opening of the SVC)',
 e:'The SA node lies in the wall of the right atrium at the junction with the superior vena cava, at the upper end of the crista terminalis.'},
{t:'v',s:'cvs',q:'Which wave of the ECG represents ventricular repolarisation?',
 a:['t wave','t','the t wave'],m:'T wave',
 e:'The T wave is ventricular repolarisation. Atrial repolarisation is hidden in the QRS complex. Peaked T waves are an early sign of hyperkalaemia.'},
/* ---- respiratory ---- */
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'A girl with an asthma attack is given salbutamol. How does it relieve bronchospasm?',
 o:['Blocks M3 muscarinic receptors on airway smooth muscle','Activates β2 receptors, raising cAMP and relaxing bronchial smooth muscle','Stabilises mast cells','Blocks leukotriene receptors','Reduces airway inflammation by inhibiting gene transcription'],a:1,
 e:'Salbutamol is a short-acting β2 agonist: Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → protein kinase A → reduced myosin light-chain kinase activity and smooth muscle relaxation. Ipratropium blocks muscarinic receptors; montelukast blocks leukotriene receptors; steroids work on gene transcription.'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'At the end of a quiet expiration, a needle is inserted through the 9th intercostal space in the mid-axillary line. What space will it enter first after the parietal pleura?',
 o:['The lower lobe of the lung','The costodiaphragmatic recess of the pleural cavity','The peritoneal cavity','The oblique fissure','The mediastinum'],a:1,
 e:'In quiet breathing, the lower border of the lung is at about the 8th rib in the mid-axillary line, but the parietal pleura extends to the 10th rib. Between them lies the costodiaphragmatic recess, which contains no lung, so pleural fluid collects there and it is a safe site for a pleural tap (avoiding going too low into the abdomen).'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'How does emphysema change lung compliance, and why?',
 o:['Compliance falls because fibrosis stiffens the lung','Compliance rises because elastic tissue in alveolar walls is destroyed','Compliance falls because surfactant is lost','Compliance is unchanged; only airway resistance increases','Compliance rises because the chest wall becomes stiffer'],a:1,
 e:'Destruction of alveolar walls and their elastic fibres makes the lung easier to inflate (high compliance) but reduces elastic recoil, so airways collapse during expiration and air is trapped. Pulmonary fibrosis does the opposite.'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'In what form is most carbon dioxide carried in venous blood?',
 o:['Dissolved in plasma','Bound to the haem iron of haemoglobin','As bicarbonate ions','As carbamino compounds with plasma proteins','As carbonic acid'],a:2,
 e:'About 70% of CO₂ is carried as HCO₃⁻: carbonic anhydrase in red cells converts CO₂ + H₂O to H₂CO₃, which dissociates; HCO₃⁻ leaves the cell in exchange for Cl⁻ (chloride shift). About 20% is carbamino haemoglobin (bound to globin, not haem) and about 10% is dissolved.'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'A man with COPD is drowsy. ABG on room air: pH 7.29, PaO₂ 7.5 kPa, PaCO₂ 8.2 kPa, HCO₃⁻ 29 mmol/L. What is the best interpretation?',
 o:['Type 1 respiratory failure','Acute type 2 respiratory failure with no compensation','Acute-on-chronic type 2 respiratory failure','Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation','Fully compensated chronic type 2 respiratory failure'],a:2,
 e:'Low PaO₂ with high PaCO₂ is type 2 respiratory failure. The raised bicarbonate shows chronic renal compensation, but the pH is still acidotic, so there has been an acute rise in CO₂ on top of chronic retention. Fully compensated chronic disease would have a near-normal pH.'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'Which structure passes through the diaphragm at the level of T10?',
 o:['Inferior vena cava','Aorta','Oesophagus with the vagal trunks','Thoracic duct','Azygos vein'],a:2,
 e:'"I 8 10 Eggs At 12": IVC at T8 (with the right phrenic nerve), oesophagus at T10 (with the anterior and posterior vagal trunks), aorta at T12 (with the thoracic duct and often the azygos vein).'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'What happens to pulmonary arterioles supplying a region of lung with low alveolar oxygen?',
 o:['They dilate, as systemic arterioles do','They constrict, diverting blood to better-ventilated lung','They are unaffected because pulmonary vessels lack smooth muscle','They open arteriovenous shunts','They secrete surfactant'],a:1,
 e:'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction matches perfusion to ventilation. It is the opposite of systemic vessels. When hypoxia is widespread (high altitude, chronic lung disease), it causes pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain (cor pulmonale).'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'After a thyroidectomy, a patient\'s left recurrent laryngeal nerve is found to be cut. Which intrinsic laryngeal muscle on the left will still work?',
 o:['Posterior cricoarytenoid','Lateral cricoarytenoid','Thyroarytenoid','Cricothyroid','Transverse arytenoid'],a:3,
 e:'The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid, which is supplied by the external laryngeal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve (also at risk near the superior thyroid artery).'},
{t:'v',s:'resp',q:'Name the only muscle that abducts (opens) the vocal folds.',
 a:['posterior cricoarytenoid','posterior cricoarytenoid muscle','posterior crico arytenoid','pca'],m:'Posterior cricoarytenoid',
 e:'Posterior cricoarytenoid rotates the arytenoids to open the rima glottidis. Bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury paralyses it on both sides, which can obstruct the airway (stridor).'},
{t:'v',s:'resp',q:'Which cell type covers about 95% of the alveolar surface and forms part of the blood–air barrier?',
 a:['type 1 pneumocytes','type i pneumocytes','type 1 pneumocyte','type i pneumocyte','type 1 alveolar cells','type i alveolar cells','type 1 alveolar epithelial cells','type i alveolar epithelial cells','squamous alveolar cells'],m:'Type I pneumocytes',
 e:'Type I pneumocytes are thin squamous cells ideal for diffusion but cannot divide; type II pneumocytes make surfactant and replace type I cells after injury.'},
/* ---- GI ---- */
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A groin hernia is reduced, and pressure over the midpoint of the inguinal ligament (about 1.5 cm above it) stops it reappearing when the patient coughs. What type of hernia is this?',
 o:['Direct inguinal hernia','Indirect inguinal hernia','Femoral hernia','Spigelian hernia','Obturator hernia'],a:1,
 e:'Pressure over the deep inguinal ring controls an indirect hernia, which enters the inguinal canal through the deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels. A direct hernia pushes through the posterior wall (Hesselbach\'s triangle), medial to these vessels, and is not controlled by pressure over the deep ring.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A woman with acute cholecystitis has pain under the right ribs and at the tip of her right shoulder. Why does she feel pain at the shoulder?',
 o:['The gallbladder is supplied by the suprascapular nerve','The inflamed gallbladder irritates diaphragmatic peritoneum supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3–5)','Bile has leaked into the right pleural cavity','The vagus nerve refers pain to the shoulder','The right greater splanchnic nerve supplies the shoulder skin'],a:1,
 e:'Irritation of the peritoneum under the diaphragm is carried by the phrenic nerve (C3–5). The shoulder tip skin is supplied by C4 via the supraclavicular nerves, so the brain refers the pain there.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A child swallows a coin. At which level is it most likely to stick in the oesophagus?',
 o:['Where the oesophagus passes through the diaphragm (T10)','At the cricopharyngeus (C6)','Where it is crossed by the aortic arch (T4)','Where the left main bronchus crosses it (T5)','At the gastro-oesophageal junction'],a:1,
 e:'The upper oesophageal sphincter at cricopharyngeus (C6) is the narrowest point, so most swallowed foreign bodies in children lodge there. The other narrowings are where the aortic arch and left main bronchus cross and at the diaphragmatic hiatus.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'Which two veins unite to form the hepatic portal vein behind the neck of the pancreas?',
 o:['Superior mesenteric and splenic veins','Inferior mesenteric and splenic veins','Left gastric and splenic veins','Superior and inferior mesenteric veins','Splenic and left renal veins'],a:0,
 e:'The superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein join behind the neck of the pancreas, at about L1/L2. The inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into the splenic vein first.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'During a laparotomy, the surgeon examines a loop of small bowel with a thick wall, prominent circular folds, long vasa recta and only one or two arterial arcades. Which part of the bowel is it?',
 o:['Duodenum','Jejunum','Ileum','Caecum','Sigmoid colon'],a:1,
 e:'Jejunum: thicker, wider, redder, with prominent plicae circulares, long vasa recta and few arcades, and less fat in its mesentery. Ileum: thinner wall, short vasa recta, many arcades, more mesenteric fat and Peyer\'s patches.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A man with alcoholic cirrhosis has a prolonged prothrombin time that does not correct fully with intravenous vitamin K. Why?',
 o:['Vitamin K is not absorbed in cirrhosis','The liver cannot synthesise enough clotting factors','Platelets are destroyed in the enlarged spleen','There is too much antithrombin','Bile acid excess inactivates clotting factors'],a:1,
 e:'Factors II, VII, IX and X are made by hepatocytes. In obstructive jaundice (vitamin K malabsorption) the PT corrects with IV vitamin K; in cirrhosis the synthetic machinery itself has failed, so it does not. INR is therefore a marker of liver synthetic function.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A newborn fails to pass meconium in the first 48 hours and has a distended abdomen. Biopsy of the rectum shows no ganglion cells. What has gone wrong in development?',
 o:['Failure of the midgut to rotate','Failed migration of neural crest cells into the distal bowel wall','Failure of the vitelline duct to close','Atresia of the duodenum','Failure of the urorectal septum to form'],a:1,
 e:'Hirschsprung\'s disease: neural crest cells fail to reach the distal colon, so the myenteric and submucosal plexuses are absent there. The aganglionic segment cannot relax, causing functional obstruction with dilated bowel above it.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A student gets bloating, flatulence and diarrhoea after drinking milk. What is the mechanism?',
 o:['An IgE-mediated allergy to milk protein','Undigested lactose draws water into the bowel osmotically and is fermented by colonic bacteria','Autoimmune damage to villi caused by gluten','Lack of pancreatic lipase','Excess gastric acid'],a:1,
 e:'Lactase on the brush border normally splits lactose into glucose and galactose. Without it, lactose stays in the lumen, holding water (osmotic diarrhoea), and colonic bacteria ferment it to gas and short-chain fatty acids.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A squamous cell carcinoma arises in the anal canal below the pectinate line. To which nodes will it first spread?',
 o:['Internal iliac nodes','Inferior mesenteric nodes','Superficial inguinal nodes','Para-aortic nodes','Sacral nodes'],a:2,
 e:'Below the pectinate line the anal canal is derived from ectoderm, with somatic (pudendal) nerves, inferior rectal vessels and lymph drainage to the superficial inguinal nodes. Above the line, lymph goes to internal iliac and inferior mesenteric nodes.'},
{t:'v',s:'gi',q:'Name the double fold of peritoneum that connects the lesser curvature of the stomach to the liver.',
 a:['lesser omentum','the lesser omentum','hepatogastric ligament','lesser omentum hepatogastric ligament'],m:'Lesser omentum',
 e:'The lesser omentum has two parts: the hepatogastric ligament and the hepatoduodenal ligament, whose free edge holds the portal triad (bile duct, hepatic artery proper, portal vein) and forms the front of the epiploic foramen.'},
{t:'v',s:'gi',q:'Which hormone stimulates pancreatic duct cells to secrete bicarbonate when acid enters the duodenum?',
 a:['secretin'],m:'Secretin',
 e:'Secretin from duodenal S cells drives bicarbonate secretion (through CFTR) to neutralise gastric acid, protecting the duodenum and providing the right pH for pancreatic enzymes.'},
/* ---- renal ---- */
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'A 60-year-old man presents with a new left-sided varicocele that does not empty when he lies down. Imaging shows a tumour of the left kidney. Why does the tumour cause a varicocele?',
 o:['The left testicular artery arises from the left renal artery','The left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein, which the tumour obstructs','The tumour compresses the inguinal canal','Lymph from the testis drains to the renal hilum','The left testicular vein drains into the IVC'],a:1,
 e:'The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein; the right drains directly into the IVC. Tumour invading or compressing the left renal vein raises pressure in the pampiniform plexus. A varicocele that persists when lying down needs investigation.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'The clearance of which substance gives the most accurate measure of glomerular filtration rate?',
 o:['Creatinine','Urea','Inulin','Para-aminohippuric acid (PAH)','Glucose'],a:2,
 e:'Inulin is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so its clearance equals GFR. Creatinine is slightly secreted, so creatinine clearance overestimates GFR. PAH is almost completely cleared in one pass, so it measures renal plasma flow. Glucose is fully reabsorbed.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'A patient on a diuretic for hypertension is found to have a raised plasma calcium. Which diuretic is most likely?',
 o:['Furosemide','Bendroflumethiazide','Spironolactone','Amiloride','Acetazolamide'],a:1,
 e:'Thiazides block NCC in the distal tubule and increase calcium reabsorption there, which can cause mild hypercalcaemia (and are used to reduce calcium stones). Loop diuretics increase calcium excretion.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'Which part of the nephron is impermeable to water and actively pumps NaCl into the medullary interstitium, creating the concentration gradient?',
 o:['Proximal convoluted tubule','Thin descending limb of the loop of Henle','Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle','Collecting duct','Bowman\'s capsule'],a:2,
 e:'The thick ascending limb uses NKCC2 to move NaCl out but has no aquaporins, so it dilutes the tubular fluid and concentrates the interstitium. This is the "single effect" of the countercurrent multiplier.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'A patient with heart failure starts spironolactone and later has a plasma potassium of 6.1 mmol/L. How does spironolactone cause this?',
 o:['It blocks NKCC2 in the loop of Henle','It blocks mineralocorticoid receptors in collecting duct principal cells, reducing Na⁺ reabsorption and K⁺ secretion','It inhibits carbonic anhydrase','It increases renin secretion','It blocks aquaporin-2'],a:1,
 e:'Aldosterone acting on mineralocorticoid receptors increases ENaC (Na⁺ in) and ROMK (K⁺ out) in principal cells. Spironolactone blocks this, so K⁺ is retained. The risk is higher with ACE inhibitors and in kidney disease.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'Which nerves cause the detrusor muscle to contract during voiding?',
 o:['Hypogastric nerves (T11–L2) via β3 receptors','Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–4) via muscarinic M3 receptors','Pudendal nerve (S2–4) via nicotinic receptors','Obturator nerve (L2–4)','Ilioinguinal nerve (L1)'],a:1,
 e:'Parasympathetic pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–4) contract the detrusor through M3 receptors. Sympathetic hypogastric nerves relax the detrusor (β3) and close the bladder neck (α1). The somatic pudendal nerve controls the external urethral sphincter.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'A child has heavy proteinuria and generalised oedema. Electron microscopy shows effacement of the cells whose foot processes and slit diaphragms form the outer layer of the filtration barrier. Which cells are these?',
 o:['Mesangial cells','Glomerular endothelial cells','Podocytes','Juxtaglomerular cells','Parietal epithelial cells of Bowman\'s capsule'],a:2,
 e:'Podocytes (visceral epithelial cells) wrap the glomerular capillaries; slit diaphragms containing nephrin between their foot processes are a key barrier to albumin. Effacement of foot processes is the hallmark of minimal change disease, the commonest cause of nephrotic syndrome in children.'},
{t:'v',s:'renal',q:'Which vessel carries blood away from the glomerulus?',
 a:['efferent arteriole','the efferent arteriole','efferent'],m:'Efferent arteriole',
 e:'Blood enters through the afferent arteriole and leaves through the efferent arteriole, which then forms the peritubular capillaries or vasa recta. Constriction of the efferent arteriole raises glomerular capillary pressure and GFR.'},
{t:'v',s:'renal',q:'Name the group of specialised tubular cells at the end of the thick ascending limb that sense NaCl delivery and signal to the juxtaglomerular cells.',
 a:['macula densa','the macula densa','macula densa cells'],m:'Macula densa',
 e:'The macula densa detects NaCl in the tubular fluid. Low NaCl stimulates renin release and afferent dilation; high NaCl constricts the afferent arteriole (tubuloglomerular feedback).'},
/* ---- endocrine ---- */
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'A 48-year-old woman has fatigue, weight gain and cold intolerance. TSH is 18 mU/L (high) and free T4 is low. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
 o:['Secondary hypothyroidism from pituitary failure','Primary hypothyroidism','Graves\' disease','Sick euthyroid syndrome','Subclinical hyperthyroidism'],a:1,
 e:'A high TSH with low T4 means the thyroid itself is failing and the pituitary is trying to drive it (primary hypothyroidism). In the UK the commonest cause is Hashimoto\'s thyroiditis (anti-TPO antibodies). In secondary hypothyroidism both TSH and T4 are low or TSH is inappropriately normal.'},
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'A patient has features of Cushing\'s syndrome. Which pattern indicates a cortisol-secreting adrenal adenoma?',
 o:['High cortisol, high ACTH','High cortisol, low ACTH','Low cortisol, high ACTH','Low cortisol, low ACTH','Normal cortisol, high ACTH'],a:1,
 e:'An adrenal adenoma secretes cortisol autonomously; negative feedback suppresses ACTH (ACTH-independent Cushing\'s). Pituitary Cushing\'s disease and ectopic ACTH (e.g. small-cell lung cancer) have high ACTH. Exogenous steroids also give low ACTH.'},
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'A 35-year-old woman has weight loss, fatigue, low blood pressure and darkened skin in her palmar creases and gums. Why is her skin pigmented?',
 o:['Excess cortisol stimulates melanocytes','High ACTH, made from POMC, activates melanocortin receptors on melanocytes','Low aldosterone causes melanin retention','Haemosiderin deposition from high iron','Excess adrenal androgens'],a:1,
 e:'In primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison\'s disease), low cortisol removes negative feedback, so the pituitary makes more POMC, which is cleaved to ACTH and α-MSH. ACTH also stimulates melanocortin-1 receptors on melanocytes. Pituitary causes of adrenal insufficiency do not cause pigmentation.'},
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'A 19-year-old with type 1 diabetes stops his insulin during a stomach bug and develops ketoacidosis. Why are ketones produced?',
 o:['Insulin deficiency increases glycogen synthesis in the liver','Without insulin, lipolysis releases fatty acids, and hepatic β-oxidation produces more acetyl-CoA than the TCA cycle can use','Glucagon is suppressed','The kidneys stop excreting ketones','Muscle breakdown releases ketone bodies directly'],a:1,
 e:'Insulin normally suppresses hormone-sensitive lipase. Without it (and with high glucagon), fatty acids flood to the liver, where β-oxidation produces acetyl-CoA that is converted to acetoacetate and β-hydroxybutyrate. These acids cause a high anion gap metabolic acidosis.'},
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'Carbimazole is used to treat Graves\' disease. Which step does it block?',
 o:['Iodide uptake by the sodium–iodide symporter','Iodination and coupling by thyroid peroxidase','Release of T4 from thyroglobulin','Peripheral conversion of T4 to T3','TSH receptor activation'],a:1,
 e:'Thionamides (carbimazole, propylthiouracil) inhibit thyroid peroxidase, which oxidises iodide, iodinates tyrosine residues and couples them into T3 and T4. Propylthiouracil also reduces peripheral T4 → T3 conversion. Perchlorate blocks iodide uptake.'},
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'A 9-year-old has a smooth lump in the midline of the neck just below the hyoid bone. It moves upwards when she sticks out her tongue. What is it?',
 o:['Branchial cyst','Thyroglossal duct cyst','Lymph node','Thyroid nodule in the isthmus','Cystic hygroma'],a:1,
 e:'The thyroid descends from the foramen caecum of the tongue along the thyroglossal duct, which normally disappears. A remnant can form a midline cyst, attached to the tongue base, so it rises on tongue protrusion. A thyroid lump rises on swallowing; branchial cysts lie laterally at the front of sternocleidomastoid.'},
{t:'v',s:'endo',q:'Which zone of the adrenal cortex produces cortisol?',
 a:['zona fasciculata','fasciculata','the zona fasciculata'],m:'Zona fasciculata',
 e:'Zona glomerulosa (outer): aldosterone. Zona fasciculata (middle, largest): cortisol under ACTH. Zona reticularis (inner): androgens.'},
{t:'v',s:'endo',q:'Which cells of the pancreatic islets secrete glucagon?',
 a:['alpha cells','alpha','a cells','α cells','alpha cell','α'],m:'Alpha (α) cells',
 e:'α cells: glucagon; β cells: insulin; δ cells: somatostatin; PP cells: pancreatic polypeptide.'},
/* ---- reproductive ---- */
{t:'s',s:'repro',q:'In a 28-day cycle without pregnancy, what directly triggers menstruation?',
 o:['A rise in FSH','The LH surge','A fall in progesterone (and oestrogen) as the corpus luteum regresses','A rise in hCG','A rise in inhibin'],a:2,
 e:'The corpus luteum survives about 14 days without hCG. As it regresses, progesterone and oestrogen fall; spiral arteries constrict and the functional layer of the endometrium becomes ischaemic and is shed.'},
{t:'s',s:'repro',q:'A 14-year-old boy has sudden severe testicular pain. The cremasteric reflex is absent on that side. Which nerve carries the motor limb of this reflex?',
 o:['Ilioinguinal nerve','Genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve','Pudendal nerve','Obturator nerve','Iliohypogastric nerve'],a:1,
 e:'Stroking the inner thigh (sensory: femoral branch of genitofemoral and ilioinguinal) causes the cremaster to elevate the testis via the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1–2). Absence is a strong sign of testicular torsion, a surgical emergency.'},
{t:'s',s:'repro',q:'During a hysterectomy, which structure is at risk where it passes beneath the uterine artery about 2 cm lateral to the cervix?',
 o:['Internal iliac artery','Ureter','Obturator nerve','Round ligament','Ovarian vein'],a:1,
 e:'"Water under the bridge": the ureter passes under the uterine artery near the lateral fornix of the vagina. It can be clamped, ligated or cut when the uterine artery is tied.'},
{t:'s',s:'repro',q:'A 68-year-old man has a hard, irregular nodule felt on digital rectal examination. In which zone of the prostate do most carcinomas arise?',
 o:['Transition zone','Central zone','Peripheral zone','Anterior fibromuscular stroma','Periurethral glands'],a:2,
 e:'About 70% of prostate cancers arise in the peripheral zone, which lies posteriorly against the rectum and is palpable on DRE. Benign prostatic hyperplasia arises in the transition zone around the urethra, causing obstructive urinary symptoms.'},
{t:'v',s:'repro',q:'Which cells in the testis make testosterone in response to LH?',
 a:['leydig cells','leydig','interstitial cells','interstitial cells of leydig','leydig cell'],m:'Leydig (interstitial) cells',
 e:'Leydig cells lie between the seminiferous tubules. Testosterone acts locally (with FSH on Sertoli cells) to support spermatogenesis and systemically for male characteristics, and feeds back on LH.'},
/* ---- immune & lymphatic ---- */
{t:'s',s:'imm',q:'Which cell picks up antigen in the tissues, migrates in lymph and presents it to naïve T cells in the lymph node?',
 o:['Neutrophil','Dendritic cell','Mast cell','Natural killer cell','Plasma cell'],a:1,
 e:'Dendritic cells are the most effective antigen-presenting cells for naïve T cells. They take up antigen, mature, travel in afferent lymph to the paracortex and present peptide on MHC class II (to CD4 cells) and MHC class I (to CD8 cells, by cross-presentation).'},
{t:'s',s:'imm',q:'A virus-infected liver cell displays viral peptides on MHC class I. Which cell recognises and kills it?',
 o:['CD4 helper T cell','CD8 cytotoxic T cell','B cell','Eosinophil','Regulatory T cell'],a:1,
 e:'MHC class I is on all nucleated cells and presents peptides from inside the cell to CD8 cytotoxic T cells, which kill with perforin and granzymes. MHC class II is on antigen-presenting cells and is recognised by CD4 cells.'},
{t:'s',s:'imm',q:'A year after axillary node clearance, a woman has a swollen arm that pits at first and later becomes firm. Why has fluid accumulated?',
 o:['Raised venous pressure from axillary vein thrombosis','Reduced lymph drainage leaves protein-rich fluid in the interstitium, raising interstitial oncotic pressure','Loss of plasma albumin','Increased capillary permeability from infection','Heart failure'],a:1,
 e:'Lymphatics normally return the small net filtrate and leaked protein. When they are removed, protein accumulates, pulling more water into the tissue, and chronic inflammation and fibrosis make the swelling firm and non-pitting over time. Cellulitis risk rises.'},
{t:'s',s:'imm',q:'A man stung by a wasp develops wheeze, facial swelling and hypotension within minutes. What is the immediate mechanism?',
 o:['IgG immune complexes depositing in vessels','Cross-linking of IgE on mast cells causing histamine release','Cytotoxic T cells attacking the skin','Complement activation by IgM against red cells','Delayed T-cell (type IV) hypersensitivity'],a:1,
 e:'Anaphylaxis is type I hypersensitivity. A previously sensitised person has IgE bound to mast cells and basophils; the allergen cross-links it, releasing histamine, leukotrienes and other mediators that cause vasodilation, capillary leak and bronchospasm. Treatment is IM adrenaline.'},
{t:'v',s:'imm',q:'Name the aggregates of lymphoid follicles in the mucosa of the ileum.',
 a:['peyers patches','peyer patches','peyer\'s patches','peyers patch','peyer\'s patch','peyer patch'],m:'Peyer\'s patches',
 e:'Peyer\'s patches are gut-associated lymphoid tissue. M cells in the overlying epithelium sample antigens from the lumen and pass them to dendritic cells beneath.'},
/* ---- blood ---- */
{t:'s',s:'blood',q:'A 34-year-old woman with heavy periods has Hb 92 g/L and MCV 71 fL. Which other result best confirms the likely cause?',
 o:['Raised ferritin','Low ferritin','Raised serum B12','Low reticulocyte haemoglobin with high MCV','Raised bilirubin'],a:1,
 e:'Microcytic anaemia with heavy menstrual blood loss is most likely iron deficiency. Low ferritin (iron stores) confirms it; transferrin/TIBC is high. Ferritin is an acute-phase protein, so it can be falsely normal in inflammation.'},
{t:'s',s:'blood',q:'How does unfractionated heparin produce its anticoagulant effect?',
 o:['Inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase','Binds and greatly enhances the activity of antithrombin, inactivating thrombin and factor Xa','Irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1','Activates plasminogen','Blocks the platelet P2Y12 receptor'],a:1,
 e:'Heparin binds antithrombin, speeding its inhibition of thrombin (IIa) and Xa about 1000-fold. Unfractionated heparin is monitored with the APTT and reversed with protamine. Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase; aspirin inhibits COX-1; clopidogrel blocks P2Y12.'},
{t:'v',s:'blood',q:'What is the name of the immature red cell, still containing some RNA, whose count rises a few days after a large bleed?',
 a:['reticulocyte','reticulocytes'],m:'Reticulocyte',
 e:'Reticulocytes have lost their nucleus but still contain ribosomal RNA. A raised reticulocyte count shows the marrow is responding to blood loss or haemolysis.'},
/* ---- skin ---- */
{t:'s',s:'skin',q:'A split-thickness skin graft is taken from a patient\'s thigh. How does the donor site heal without a graft of its own?',
 o:['By contraction of the wound edges only','By migration of keratinocytes from hair follicles and sweat glands left in the remaining dermis','By fibroblasts turning into keratinocytes','By melanocytes dividing to form new epidermis','It does not heal and must be grafted'],a:1,
 e:'A split-thickness graft takes the epidermis and part of the dermis. The remaining dermis contains the deeper parts of hair follicles and sweat glands, which are lined by epithelium; these cells spread over the surface and re-epithelialise the donor site in about 2 weeks. Full-thickness wounds lack these and cannot do this.'}
]});
